Congressman Bradley Byrne (R-AL), a former labor and employment attorney, joined a group of bipartisan lawmakers today in introducing legislation to make sweeping reforms to the way sexual harassment and other employment law claims are handled on Capitol Hill.
The bill, known as the Congressional Accountability Act of 1995 Reform Act (CAA Reform Act), would make a number of reforms to ensure the process is fair, transparent, and efficient. The bill also includes a number of reforms to hold Members of Congress accountable for their actions and ensure taxpayers are not left on the hook to pay for sexual and other harassment and discrimination claims.
Congressman Byrne said: I am very proud to have played a role in drafting a strong, bipartisan bill to reform the way allegations of sexual harassment and other employment law claims are handled on Capitol Hill. Congress should have to play by the same rules as the rest of America, and this bill makes sure that is the case.
Our bill ensures that individuals who make claims of sexual harassment or employment discrimination are given a timely and fair process, as well as access to qualified legal counsel and advice. Just as important, the bill will increase accountability and transparency so the American people are informed about the actions of their Member of Congress and are no longer left to foot the bill for sexual harassment settlements. 
I want to thank the bipartisan group of lawmakers who joined me in this effort, and I look forward to the Houses consideration of this legislation in the coming weeks. 
Last November, Congressman Byrne testified before the House Administration Committee to share his ideas about reforming the way sexual harassment and other employment law claims are handled on Capitol Hill. Among his recommendations that were included in the final bill were:
 Requiring mandatory harassment training for all Members of Congress and staff;
	Ensuring all House offices have an anti-harassment/anti-discrimination policy;
	Making the Congressional Accountability Act (CAA) process mirror what private businesses are subject to through the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC);
	Giving investigatory authority to the Office of Compliance (OOC);
	Not requiring all claimants to go through mandatory mediation and counseling;
	Subjecting unpaid interns, pages, fellows, etc. to the CAAs anti-discrimination provisions;
	Making Members of Congress personally financially responsible for paying any settlements or awards associated for a harassment or discrimination claim the member committed;
	Publishing information regarding sexual harassment and other employment law claims and settlements;
	Prohibiting Members of Congress from engaging in a sexual relationship with members of their staff; and
	Making Member-on-Member sexual harassment a violation of the Official Code of Conduct.
 The bill is co-sponsored by Representatives Gregg Harper (R-MS), Robert Brady (D-PA), Rodney Davis (R-IL), Barbara Comstock (R-VA), Mark Walker (R-NC), Adrian Smith (R-NE), Barry Loudermilk (R-GA), Zoe Lofgren (D-CA), Jamie Raskin (D-MD), Jackie Speier (D-CA), Susan Brooks (R-IN), and Ted Deutch (D-FL).
